roposal.md\n+++ b/docs/proposal.md\n@@ -361,7 +361,7 @@ A worker implements `plan.md` on its own branch and ends with a draft PR, test r\n 5. **Guard the diff** after each agent or fix step, before anything leaves the host. The worktree belongs to the agent, so the guard never trusts its `.git`, config, index or attributes: git runs against the controller's own clone with a private index filled from the base commit (so `skip-worktree` and `assume-unchanged` hide nothing, and no planted filter, textconv, fsmonitor or hook runs), with its objects in a private temp directory (the clone is only read, so an agent cannot grow it); a worktree with a new or resized file over 1 MiB, or more than 256 of them, is held before anything is hashed. The scanned bytes are copied to a controller-private directory and the code lane commits the copies, so nothing can change a file between the scan and the commit. Close-out's changed-file listing uses the same hardened path. Paths under `.ard/<issue#>/` and `.ard-input/` belong to close-out and the loop and are left out. Everything else is checked, and any rejection holds the job for a human (`ard:needs-human`, nothing pushed): the path rule in `committer.CheckCodePath` (all of `.github/`, the rest of `.ard/`, `.git` segments, `.gitmodules`, any `.gitattributes` (`linguist-generated` or `-diff` would collapse files in the GitHub PR diff, so a worker change to one always goes to a human), secret-shaped names), any symlink, nested repository or other non-regular file, a file over 1 MiB, a binary file, and more than 256 changed files. The credential scan runs twice: the content of every written file is checked for the values of known secrets (the hold names the variable, never the value), and the built-in pattern scanner runs over it with only the findings on lines this job added kept, so a worker that edits a file that already holds a credential-shaped fixture is not held for lines it did not write. The comment names file, line and rule (`internal/foo_test.go:12: secret scan rule aws-access-key`), never the text. A scanner error fails closed. Test fixtures that look like credentials must be built at run time, as `internal/redact`'s tests do (`\"AK\" + \"IA\" + ...`); a literal is held, and there is no in-band escape. The changed lines of the whole PR (the diff against the merge base with `main`) are checked against two per-worker-role thresholds. Over `diff_max_lines` (default 3,000) also holds, as `diff guard: N changed lines, limit M`. Over `diff_warn_lines` (default 2,000) but not over the limit, the PR goes ahead: the loop logs `worker.diff_large` (lines, warn, max), and the PR body and the close-out issue comment carry a fixed note, `Large change: N changed lines (warn at W)`, so reviewers know; the note quotes nothing from the change. The latest step's count decides, so a fix round that shrinks the PR drops the note. The comment shows a rejection reason as `diff guard: <entry>; <entry>; <entry>; and N more`.\n 6. **Two commits per round.** The loop commits the code through the committer's code lane (`Branches.CommitCode`) with the title `[<type>: ]<issue title> (#<issue>)`, the redacted summary (secrets and host paths) as the body and an `Ard-Job: <job-id>` trailer, no `[skip ci]`. `<type>` comes from the issue's `type:*` label (`feature` is `feat`, `bug` is `fix`; `docs`, `chore`, `refactor` and `test` keep their names) and with no `type:*` label the `<type>: ` part is dropped and the title is `<issue title> (#<issue>)`. The prefix is never taken from the plan or the lead's routing, so it depends on a maintainer applying `type:*` at intake. At the end close-out commits `worker-<role>.md` and the redacted logs of every agent step to the same branch (or to `main`, as triage's are, when the job stopped before its first push). `.ard/<issue#>/` never goes through the code lane. A push failure (branch exists, conflict, branch gone, guard) fails the job with no automatic retry.\n 7. **Test.** Run `tests` (default `make test`; empty disables the step) as `/bin/sh -c` in the role's image, with no credential. On failure the agent gets the failure output in a fix step, which commits and pushes again, up to `max_test_fixes` (default 2) within the same job. A fix that changes no files, the cap, or the time running out ends the loop with the tests failed. Test logs stay on the host (kept 7 days) and are never committed.\n-8. **Open or update a draft PR** with ard's own `Closes #<issue>`, the agent summary quoted and neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`, then marker and mention defusing), the test result and links to `worker-<role>.md`, the logs and the job id. Reviewers are chosen by `config.SelectReviewers`: the candidates are the role's `reviewers` (a list that only narrows), or every reviewer role when it is unset or empty, and each candidate's `when:` still decides. A `when:` matches when any path changed between the merge base of the config commit and the pushed head matches a `paths` entry, or a listed triage role ran (closed `done`, or its `ard:triage:<role>:done` label; `needs-human` does not count). Paths are case-sensitive and anchored at the repo root: `web/` is a directory, anything else a doublestar glob, so `**/go.mod` matches a root `go.mod` and `go.mod` only the root one; `.ard/` and `.ard-input/` paths are never selectable. Any `review:<role>` the lead deferred or the worker's own `next` lists is added regardless of `when:`, limited to roles that exist, and requested only when the tests did not fail. Selection is monotonic: each run recomputes from the whole PR diff and close-out only adds labels, so earlier requests stay and newly matching reviewers are added. When the head or git is unavailable, path rules fail open. If no reviewer is selected the PR stays a draft (the loop logs `worker.no_reviewers`). The PR stays a draft; the merger marks it ready. A job that has pushed always gets its draft PR, even when it then ends in `ard:needs-human` or a