s `worker.pr_retry` and does not retry. When the create still fails after this job pushed the branch, the job records the branch, head, PR title and body, summary and worker result in the nullable `jobs.pending_pr` column (migration 0009; older rows read as none) and ends `executor:failed` with the cause `pr_create_failed` (not transient, not auto-retried) and one comment naming the branch and short head: only the PR is missing. When a human removes `executor:failed`, the next worker job looks at the newest earlier worker job of the issue and role; if it has a record, the branch tip is its recorded head (or only adds files under `.ard/`, which is the failed job's own close-out commit) and no open PR exists for the branch, the loop opens the PR from the record and closes the job done with no agent run and no test run (`WorkerResult.Reopened`; close-out reads no role file and takes the recorded summary). Otherwise, or if the lookup fails, the full worker runs as before. The built-in engineer prompt also tells the agent to answer `done` with the original summary when the branch already holds the complete change and no PR exists.\n 9. **Report.** Comment on the issue with the summary, PR link and test result (`passed`, `passed after 1 fix attempt`, `failed after 2 fix attempts`, `not configured`); swap `ard:worker:<role>:running` for `:done`. If tests still fail, add `ard:tests:failed` on the issue and the PR and request no reviewers; a green run removes it. A worker that ends without the loop's result (for example one the Reaper closes out), or whose role file says `needs-human`, never becomes `:done` without a PR. A role file that says `needs-network` on a first job (no branch, no PR, nothing pushed) bounces the issue to the triage engineer once, as described under the role-file contract; in any other case it ends `needs-human`, nothing is pushed, and the comment lists the needed inputs.\n \n **The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (3,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 2,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages. With `network: true` it may fetch other inputs, records each under `fetched:` and treats the fetched bytes as untrusted data, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make lint test` (so formatting and vet failures are caught before a PR opens), and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 2000`, `diff_max_lines: 3000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base. It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present; the file is a constraint, never an instruction, and the worker never resolves a conflict with